ities in the inequality above, except for $\\Vert \\mathbf{u} \\Vert.$ So first, we compute $\\Vert \\mathbf{u} \\Vert$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖u‖ & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(\\sqrt{k})^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6+k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|u\\cdot v| & \\le ‖u‖\\cdot ‖v‖ \\\\ |-12| & \\le \\sqrt{6+k}\\cdot (4) \\\\ 12 & \\le 4\\sqrt{6+k} \\\\ 3 & \\le \\sqrt{6+k} \\\\ {3}^{2} & \\le (\\sqrt{6+k})^{2} \\\\ 9 & \\le 6+k \\\\ 3 & \\le k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the smallest possible value of $k$ is $3.$" 13194139535014 true] [17592186133504 1100 "Find the acute angle between the vectors ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14046175/assets/5ed21f4bb536757026315cce838f4cc3028493adac2ff29d46a5b8cbdf11170b.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14046175/assets/8d813e104a708be37ba44b9448806f49ea21a16fe7f613314d2eeab2b59bbda2.png)" 13194139535014 true] [17592186133504 1100 "Find the acute angle between the vectors $\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139535014 false] [17592186133504 1145 "Using the formula\n\n$$\n\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{\\mathbf{v} \\cdot \\mathbf{w}}{\\Vert\\mathbf{v}\\Vert \\, \\Vert \\mathbf{w}\\Vert}\n$$\n\nwe have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cos θ &= \\frac{v_{1}w_{1} + v_{2}w_{2} + v_{3}w_{3} + v_{4}w_{4}}{\\sqrt{v_{1}^{2} + v_{2}^{2} + v_{3}^{2} + v_{4}^{2}}\\sqrt{w_{1}^{2} + w_{2}^{2} + w_{3}^{2} + w_{4}^{2}}} \\\\\n&= ((-1) \\cdot (-2) + 2 \\cdot 2 + (-4) \\cdot (-2) + (-2) \\cdot 2)/(\\sqrt{(-1)^{2} + 2^{2} + (-4)^{2} + (-2)^{2}}\\sqrt{(-2)^{2} + 2^{2} + (-2)^{2} + 2^{2}}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{10}{\\sqrt{25} \\cdot \\sqrt{16}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{10}{5 \\cdot 4} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta=\\arccos \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2} \\right)=\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\n$$" 13194139535014 false] [17592186133504 1145 "Using the formula\n\n$$\n\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{\\mathbf{v} \\cdot \\mathbf{w}}{\\Vert\\mathbf{v}\\Vert \\, \\Vert \\mathbf{w}\\Vert},\n$$\n\nwe have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{{v}_{1}{w}_{1}+{v}_{2}{w}_{2}+{v}_{3}{w}_{3}+{v}_{4}{w}_{4}}{\\sqrt{{v}_{1}^{2}+{v}_{2}^{2}+{v}_{3}^{2}+{v}_{4}^{2}}\\,\\sqrt{{w}_{1}^{2}+{w}_{2}^{2}+{w}_{3}^{2}+{w}_{4}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1)\\cdot (-2)+2\\cdot 2+(-4)\\cdot (-2)+(-2)\\cdot 2}{\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-4)^{2}+(-2)^{2}}\\,\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{\\sqrt{25}\\cdot \\sqrt{16}} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{5\\cdot 4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta=\\arccos \\left( \\dfrac{1}{2} \\right)=\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}.\n$$" 13194139535014 true] [17592186133554 1100 "Let $A(-2, 4, -2, 2)$ and $B(4, 0, -1, -4)$. Find the cosine of the angle $\\angle{AOB}$, where $O(0,0,0,0)$ is the origin." 13194139535014 false] [17592186133554 1100 "Let $A(-2, 4, -2, 2)$ and $B(4, 0, -1, -4).$ Find the cosine of the angle $\\angle{AOB},$ where $O(0,0,0,0)$ is the origin." 13194139535014 true] [17592186133554 1145 "First, let\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a=\\overset{\\to }{OA}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,b=\\overset{\\to }{OB}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, let $\\theta = m\\angle{AOB}$ be the angle between the vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}.$ Then, we can find $\\cos \\theta$ using the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{‖a‖\\,‖b‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{1}{b}_{1}+{a}_{2}{b}_{2}+{a}_{3}{b}_{3}+{a}_{4}{b}_{4}}{\\sqrt{{a}_{1}^{2}+{a}_{2}^{2}+{a}_{3}^{2}+{a}_{4}^{2}}\\,\\sqrt{{b}_{1}^{2}+{b}_{2}^{2}+{b}_{3}^{2}+{b}_{4}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-2)\\cdot 4+4\\cdot 0+(-2)\\cdot (-1)+2\\cdot (-4)}{\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+{4}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{